For years I wrote under the name **OJ Develops** on Blogger. It was free, it was easy to start, and for a long time that was enough.

Then the blog started to work against me. I wanted a site that looked like *my* site — something I could point to from GitHub and LinkedIn without a second brand in the way. Blogger is fine for publishing, but it fights you when you want control. Themes are awkward to customize. The old custom domain is gone. And as a portfolio, a default Blogger URL never quite said what I wanted it to say.

So I moved.

This site, **ojraqueno.com**, is the new home. It is a static site. I write posts locally, commit them to git, and they go live. If I want the look to change, I change it. No dashboard of mystery options, no waiting on a theme that almost works.

## What about the old posts?

They are here too.

I imported the public Blogger archive and kept the original publish dates. Each migrated post has a short note that links back to the Blogger original. I am not going to pretend those posts were written yesterday on this domain.

If you still have the Blogger RSS feed bookmarked, keep it for a while. New writing will land here first.

## What I plan to write about

The topics will be familiar if you have been reading for a while: how we structure .NET applications, full-stack work, and the design decisions that show up once a codebase has been alive for more than a sprint.

I will still write how-tos when I think they are useful. I also want to write more about tradeoffs: when *not* to add a layer, how AI changes the day-to-day without replacing judgment, and Azure as something I *use* to ship rather than as an admin specialty.

One more change in how I think about the blog: posts and companion GitHub repos are one thing. If an idea needs code, the code should sit next to the post, not live as a screenshot that goes stale.

## Conclusion

In this post I talked about why I left Blogger and what this site is for. The old archive is here, the dates are the real dates, and new posts will show up on ojraqueno.com.

If you have been reading since the Blogger days, thank you — and welcome to the new URL. If you are here for the first time, start with the [blog](/blog/) and poke around.
